Holtkeeper Secret Reports License

Copyright (c) 2026 Holtkeeper contributors

This license covers the files of the `@holtkeeper/secret-reports` package (the directory that
holds this file) and the parts of Holtkeeper that serve its reports: the
`report_secret_exposure` MCP tool, the secret report routes of the server and the pages of the
panel that show reports or let people choose how they report (together, "the Feature"). The
rest of Holtkeeper stays under the MIT License in the repository root.

Permission is hereby granted, free of charge, to any person obtaining a copy of this software
and associated documentation files (the "Software"), to deal in the Software without
restriction, including without limitation the rights to use, copy, modify, merge, publish,
distribute, sublicense, and/or sell copies of the Software, and to permit persons to whom the
Software is furnished to do so, subject to the following conditions:

1. The above copyright notice, this permission notice and the Use Condition below shall be
   included in all copies or substantial portions of the Software.

2. Use Condition. An organization may run the Feature (keep it enabled in a deployment that
   people other than the organization's own administrators use) only if all of the following
   hold for as long as it runs:

   a. Voluntary use. Every person decides for themselves whether their agents report exposed
      secrets and whether reports carry their name. No one is required, asked as a condition of
      employment, contract or access, or pressured to enable the Feature or to report under
      their name, and no one is disadvantaged for turning it off or reporting anonymously.

   b. No sanctions. Reports, their content, their absence, and the reporting choice a person
      made are never used, alone or together with other information, as grounds for dismissal,
      disciplinary action, performance or compensation decisions, or any other adverse action
      against the person who reported, the person whose mistake led to the exposure, or anyone
      else. Reports serve one purpose: finding and rotating exposed secrets.

   c. No identification. No one tries to find out who sent an anonymous report or who caused an
      exposure, whether by correlating logs, access records, timestamps or other data, or by
      asking people.

   d. Notice. The people who use the deployment can read this Use Condition. The Software shows
      it next to the reporting choice; removing or hiding that notice is not permitted.

3. An organization that does not meet the Use Condition must turn the Feature off (for example
   with `server.secretReports: false`) or stop using the Software. Running the Feature while
   breaking the Use Condition is not licensed, and the permissions above end for that
   organization until it meets the condition again.

